# E0139: operator on a value of a type parameter

An operator on a value whose type is a type parameter `T`, or names one, as `[T]` does, needs a bound. `Eq` gives `==` and `!=`, and `Ord` gives `<`, `<=`, `>` and `>=`. `contains` on a `[T]` compares elements with `==`, so it needs `T: Eq`. No bound gives `+` or `-`.

Comparing an optional with `None` is allowed: on a `T?` or a `[T]?`, `x == None` and `x != None` test only whether a value is there, and need nothing from `T`. Comparing the values inside, as `x == y` on two `T?` does, needs `T: Eq`.

## Why

`T` may stand for any type, and not every type has every operator: a class has no `<`, and an array has no `+`. A bound says that a type conforms to an interface, and the interfaces `Eq` and `Ord` give the operators. A generic function can use only what its bounds give.

Operands of different types are the mistake, whatever bounds the types have. That error is [E0047](E0047.md) for `==` and `!=`, and [E0032](E0032.md) for `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=` and `contains`. This error comes when the operands are one type. Then the help shows the bound to add. It keeps the bounds that `T` has: `T: Display` gets `T: Display & Ord`. The help names a type parameter only. A class `Box<T>` has no `==` of its own, so a bound on `T` does not give one: that is [E0159](E0159.md), and `Box<T>` must conform to `Eq`. The same holds for any other part of the operand that is not a type parameter.

## Broken

```sisu
fn smaller<T>(a: T, b: T) -> bool {
    a < b
}

fn main() {}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
fn smaller<T: Ord>(a: T, b: T) -> bool {
    a < b
}

fn main() {
    print(smaller(1, 2))
}
```
